SM(375,{"n":"Numbers 27","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Numbers 27.mp3","d":2220.7,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.68,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[24.64,"And when we get started here in just a moment, we're gonna be picking up in Book of Numbers, beginning of chapter 27."],[34.32,"So uh and it will be just a moment because you don't have to do a ton of recap because we we left off last time with census data."],[42.32,"Woo! So I'm not gonna reread all those numbers uh as exciting as it was when I read them last time."],[48.88,"But we talked about why that was that we had the census at the beginning of the book."],[52.8,"Now we have this census as we're coming to the end of the book of numbers."],[56.4,"And the point being that there was a succession of generations there."],[59.6,"There was the generation that came out of Egypt that died in the wilderness, and then now there's the new generation, their children, and so they're sort of renumbered again."],[70.3,"As they're preparing to enter the land and face resistance as they do."],[76.14,"Not only are we coming off of a census, but this is also another one of those places, as we've said over and over again in the book of Numbers, Book of Numbers is sort of this patchwork quilt of different kinds of material interspersed between each other."],[89.82,"So there's narrative, there's stories, there's census data, there's commandments, and they're all kind of mixed together."],[100.38,"And so we're also switching from after the census data to a different genre."],[104.14,"to a We're gonna have a tiny little piece of narrative here, and that's gonna serve introduce bunch of legislative material, meaning a bunch of commandments related to it."],[116.5,"And then we're just gonna get commandments for a while."],[118.82,"Then we're gonna get another little piece of narrative, and then commandments again."],[121.75,"So there are only a few little morsels of narrative left in the book of numbers."],[126.79,"Most of what's left in the book of numbers is gonna be commandments in these last we've got ten chapters left."],[133.99,"But they're gonna go relatively quickly because of the type of material they are."],[138.15,"We're I don't think we're gonna get through ten chapters tonight."],[141.27,"Not that quickly, but more quickly than some of the material."],[145.75,"And so that said, we don't need a whole lot more review, I don't think, to get to where we start here in uh Numbers 27."],[157.07,"So, unless anybody has any leftover comments, questions, rodent sightings to report, uh, we'll go ahead and pick up in chapter 27, verse 1."],[168.59,"Now came the daughters of Zalopahad, the son of Hefer, the son of Gilead, the son of Makir, the son of Manasseh from the tribe of Manasseh, the son of Joseph."],[180.27,"And these were the names of his daughters: Malah, Noah, Hogla, Milkah, and Tirzah."],[188.27,"These are all perfectly good biblical names, but if you name your daughter Hogla, she will grow up and harm you."],[192.67,"So I'm just warning you now at least in an English speaking country, and for purposes of people here, I will not baptize your daughter Hogla."],[206.39,"I will change that name, and she will thank me for it."],[209.83,"But if you want Milka or uh Tirza, you know, that's fine."],[214.87,"So these are when it gives the son of, son of, son of, as we've said before, this isn't a complete list of generations."],[222.55,"This is locating them within the tribes."],[225.92,"So Manasseh is the name of the tribe, and then there's the name of the clan and the extended family and the immediate family."],[233.86,"And this is just going in ascending order instead of descending order."],[237.62,"So rather than starting at the tribe of Manasseh, it starts with, you know, these these are actually the daughters of this person, and then telling you where that person is located in the broader familial and tribal structures of Israel."],[255.02,"So is that more sense of like uh leadership in a way, like importance rather than like you said, it's not exactly it, is it when you say locating the tribes?"],[268.54,"Well, this is sort of a part of identity."],[271.55,"And w we don't, at least here in our American culture, have that level of specificity."],[283.59,"But there are a lot of countries still, like if you look at Latin, l everybody, you know, Roman citizens all had like five or six names, and it was, you know, their given name."],[294.87,"Which sometimes was lame."],[296.47,"Like there are a lot of people named like Pri Primus, Secundus, Tertius, Quadratus, you know."],[303.43,"That would be their given name, but then they had their father's name, their grandfather's name, you know, an ancestor's name."],[311.21,"They had like a whole right."],[313.69,"And there are other cultures, like I know in Ethiopia, it's like your given name, your father's name, your grandfather's name."],[319.83,"Like there's more of a sense that your identity, who you are, is located in tracing this line back."],[326.71,"And sort of these larger units"],[332.32,"And in the US, you know, you have a first name and a last name."],[337.68,"And that's about it."],[338.96,"And usually your last name is at least one of your parents' last name now in the United States."],[345.36,"But that's about it."],[346.34,"That's about as far as it works here."],[350.9,"So, yeah, so we don't quite have that same sense."],[354.1,"But in a lot of more traditional cultures, they still have this idea of, you know, like that that that sort of locates you, your identity is a function of being part of these larger units in the present time and historically."],[370.34,"So it's that it's that kind of idea"],[370.5,"So it's who are who are these women who are coming to see Moses?"],[376.64,"Oh, here's who they are."],[378.74,"Here's how they relate who was part of this family, that's part of this clan, that's part of this tribe."],[384.9,"So they stood before Moses, before Eliezer the priest, and before the rulers and all the congregation, by the door of the tabernacle of testimony, saying, Okay, so as we've seen already, there's right the gateway into the sort of tabernacle compound."],[402.82,"So there's the tabernacle, the tent itself, and then there's the curtain that sort of goes around the perimeter of that area."],[411.94,"And people who weren't priests and Levites didn't go in."],[417.66,"But when, as we've seen over and over again, when Moses had something to say, when there was some major dispute that needed to be settled, even when God wanted to address all of the people, they were all gathered to this place."],[432.64,"So this is later in as we go through the Old Testament, later in Israelite history, this is going to become the city gate."],[443.2,"So the people who live in the city and the people who live in the countryside on farms around the city, when they have business to do, they're going to go to the city gate."],[451.84,"And the city gate is where the elders of the people are going to be."],[456.24,"So if you're going to make a contract, you go there, because there are people there who can witness the contract."],[459.68,"Or if you have a dispute you need settled, you go there so the elders can settle it"],[465.45,"So when they're in the camp in the wilderness, that's the gateway of the tabernacle."],[471.13,"Okay, so they have an issue that they need resolved."],[475.21,"So these women show up there to do it."],[480.65,"They didn't gather everybody there, but that's that's the place where people gather."],[485.69,"And so they this is their issue."],[487.77,"They say, Our father died in the desert, but he was not in the company of those who gathered together against the Lord in company with Korah, but he died in his own sin and had no sons."],[500.33,"Okay, so he's one of that first generation that came out of Egypt, their dad"],[507.65,"And he wasn't part of Korah's rebellion."],[509.89,"He wasn't part of one of these other rebellions where a bunch of people died, but he just died as part of that generation."],[516.53,"That whole generation died in the wilderness because of their sin."],[520.39,"So he's he's one of those folks."],[523.59,"And he has no sons."],[527.13,"Which means end of his line, end of his inheritance."],[530.65,"Because remember, part of the reason why we had got this census data is that not just the tribes in general, but each individual family is going to be given a piece of land when they go into Canaan."],[546.65,"And that's going to be their family's land."],[549.25,"Hypothetically, if they had done what they were supposed to do every 50 years in the in the year of Jubilee, that land would revert to that family."],[558.35,"That's that's the inheritance they're talking about."],[560.59,"They're not just talking about oh, there's no one to inherit my dad's clothes and walking stick or tent."],[566.37,"This is about in the land."],[568.7,"So they say, you know, yes, that generation died, the next generation, the sons are gonna be the ones who receive that inheritance in the land, but our dad died and doesn't have a son, just us daughters."],[584.38,"So, what happens to us?"],[587.34,"That's kind of brave, yeah."],[590.06,"Ye.ah What happens to us."],[591.44,"Yeah. technically speaking, women did not have the right to address like court cases at this time in history"],[598.68,"So it's a little they they have some some chutzpah."],[602.6,"Uh four, do not let the name of our father be wiped out from among his tribe because he had no son."],[611.24,"Give us a possession among our father's brothers."],[615.18,"So they're uncles."],[616.88,"So they're uncles, they have male cousins who are gonna inherit."],[620.94,"A piece of land, they say, We we would like to have a piece of land just like them."],[626.3,"So Moses brought their case before the Lord."],[628.78,"Then the Lord spoke to Moses, saying, The daughters of Salafahed speak correctly, you shall surely give them a possession of. of inheritance among their father's brothers, and assign the allotment their father to them"],[641.86,"Then you shall speak to the children of Israel, saying, If a man dies and has no son, you shall assign his inheritance to his daughter."],[648.58,"But if he has no daughter, you shall give the inheritance to his brother."],[652.82,"But if he has no brothers, you shall give his inheritance to his father's brother."],[657.86,"But if his father has no brothers, you shall give the inheritance to the relative closest to him in his tribe, and he shall inherit his possessions."],[666.34,"So this shall be an ordinance of judgment to the children of Israel, as the Lord ordered Moses."],[671.38,"Okay So what's what's the point of this?"],[674.74,"Early feminism. Well, sort of."],[679.06,"Sort of. right In that?"],[679.89,"We do have women in inheriting things that normally they couldn't inherit"],[686.59,"But it's not just women inheriting things."],[688.53,"Because we read the rest of that too."],[690.31,"Well, what if he has no kids at all when he dies?"],[693.69,"Well, then it goes to his brother."],[694.81,"Well, what if he has no brothers?"],[696.33,"Goes to his uncle."],[697.37,"So the idea here, though, is that we are preserving this particular piece of land as a family inheritance."],[705.45,"At least, again, theoretically, if they did what they were supposed to do."],[708.73,"As separate. As separate."],[711.23,"Because if it's, say, his father's brother."],[715.75,"His father would have received the inheritance from his grandfather."],[719.19,"His brother would not have gotten a separate."],[721.69,"The land didn't keep getting subdivided."],[722.57,"It was inherited by the firstborn son"],[726.21,"So if the secondborn son inherits this piece, it's still separate."],[732.34,"So the idea here is keeping all of those inheritances separate, meaning this is God preventing another way someone could have tried to accumulate land."],[743.22,"Like, well, I'm going to arrange marriages and I'm going to do this and that."],[746.5,"I mean, and try and, you know, cobble together this huge, you know, and and I'll make the family relationshipships such that, you know, and manipulate leverett marriage and other social disusages in such a way that that land never gets taken away from me and my family, and me and my family just get more and more land. and accumulate"],[770.3,"Because that flies in the face of what God intended with the year of jubilee."],[774.7,"Remember, a big part of the year of jubilee is that this prevents the problem isn't preventing generational wealth."],[783.34,"The problem is preventing generational poverty."],[786.56,"It's not that, oh, that would be horrible if subsequent generations of a family all accumulated wealth at all prospered."],[793.92,"That's not like seen as a horrible thing."],[796.48,"But it is seen as a horrible thing if that's happening, and other families then have generational poverty."],[803.44,"Go generation after generation without having land of their own."],[807.36,"land of their own And have no way to get."],[811.64,"From generation to generation to generation."],[814.22,"And so you're just born into poverty and you're gonna live your whole life in it, and there's nothing you can do."],[819.42,"Now there is also a sort of moral hazard, one might say, even though it seems a lot less dire to us, but maybe isn't, of people being born in wealth, and there's nothing they could do to not be wealthy their entire lives."],[836.06,"That does not tend to produce the best sort of people from a on a moral basis."],[840.55,"That''ss that a general tendency we might notice in human history."],[844.76,"But that is a less important moral hazard in terms of the Torah than the opposite."],[850.62,"Then the opposite"],[851.94,"So you may make bad choices and plunge yourself into poverty for the rest of your life, but that shouldn't doom your children and your grandchildren to poverty forever, is the idea."],[863.82,"And that's what the year of jubilees was designed to prevent."],[867.18,"And so this ruling is not so much about girl power as it is about maintaining these family inheritances as separate."],[877.28,"And preventing accumulation at the potential expense of future generations of other families."],[886.24,"Verse 12. Now the Lord said to Moses, go up into the mountain beyond the Jordan."],[890.56,"This is Mount Nebo, and see the land of Canaan, which I give to the children of Israel for a possession."],[896.72,"We've already seen Moses. because of his sin striking the rock instead of speaking to it, isn't going to get to go into the land"],[905.64,"But God here tells him to go up on this mountain."],[908.2,"Remember, they're in the plains of Moab, they're on the other side of the Jordan River."],[911.64,"Tells him to go to the top of this mountain, because from there he can look down, and see the land sort of spread out."],[918.86,"Then you shall see it and be added to your people."],[921.98,"Even you, as Aaron, your brother, was added on Mount Hor."],[927.42,"Because you transgressed my word in the desert of sin."],[931.06,"When the congregation was failing to sanctify me, you did not sanctify me before them."],[937.58,"This was the water of contention at Kadesh in the desert of sin, in case you forgot."],[942.54,"Or in case it's been a couple of months since we read that chapter"],[946.42,"So, yes, God is literally saying to Moses, go up there and see the land because you're gonna die."],[952.82,"The way your brother Aaron died earlier at another mountain."],[957.54,"So Moses is made aware by God, this is it."],[962.03,"Now you're now your life is going to come to an end."],[966.51,"Notice the language."],[967.71,"This isn't just a euphemism in terms of how they talk about death."],[971.15,"You'll be added to your people."],[973.65,"So think about what that language means for a minute."],[976.77,"No, not at all."],[978.21,"Added to your people."],[979.25,"Who are who are his people?"],[980.71,"It's not the people of Israel."],[981.99,"He's already part of the people of Israel."],[984.47,"It's his ancestors."],[986.15,"It's his brother who died before him, and Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob."],[989.79,"He's going to be added to them."],[991.39,"He's going to come into some kind of community with them."],[994.67,"The reason I'm emphasizing this is that this implies."],[999.63,"Sorry, Bartherman, and certain modern secular Jews, this kind of implies an afterlife."],[1007.57,"Because none of those people are buried on that mountain."],[1010.21,"So he's not being added in that way."],[1012.53,"You could try and read it that way."],[1013.81,"Like if we're talking about Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, several generations, they're all buried in the same place."],[1018.55,"In the same set of tombs."],[1019.63,"So you could say, oh, well, it means, you know, literally, he's added to the tomb."],[1023.43,"Moses is not added to the any of their tombs."],[1027.11,"So if he's going to be joining them, he's not joining them in a tomb."],[1032.16,"He's he's joining them where they are."],[1036.02,"Which implies an afterlife, which uh of some sort."],[1040.92,"If not, I mean, we can't be specific enough, this doesn't necessarily imply the bodily resurrection or something, but it certainly implies that death is not the end of Moses' existence or the existence of his ancestors to whom he's being added."],[1053.72,"And the way it specifically points out Aaron implies also that Aaron did not just cease to exist at the point of his death a few chapters ago."],[1063.18,"And so the reason is, and this is the reason why he's not getting to enter the land."],[1068.22,"I mean, he's more than 120 years old."],[1070.72,"Like, I mean, eventually he was gonna die."],[1074.12,"But uh the reason he's not getting to enter the land is this transgression where he failed now, and notice the way it's phrased here."],[1084.12,"He failed to sanctify God before the congregation, meaning he failed to treat God as holy before the congregation."],[1092.76,"He was angry, he struck the rock, he did what he was not supposed to do."],[1097.08,"What is Moses' job as the prophet?"],[1099.08,"It's been a while since we it laid this out, but we've talked about it a bunch before."],[1103.56,"Moses' job as the prophet, what makes him the prophet is he is supposed to represent God to the people, and he's supposed to represent the people to God."],[1113.41,"So representing God to the people means he speaks with God's authority, yes, but it also means he needs to accurately represent who God is and what he says, when he when he passes that on to the people."],[1126.39,"And then the reverse, he represents the people to God in prayer and intercessions."],[1132.21,"And all of these, in all these ways."],[1135.89,"So when it says, You failed to sanctify me before the people, he's saying to Moses, this is why Moses' sin was so egregious."],[1142.69,"You think, like, okay, he hit a rock with a stick."],[1145.11,"Like, I've gotten angry and you know, thrown something on the floor."],[1150.73,"Like, that's not the most horrible thing in the world."],[1153.69,"But for Moses, whose job when he stands before the people and communicates what God has said to them and what God has told him to do, him misrepresenting God to the people is a very serious sin."],[1173.21,"Because the way the people know who God is, is Moses."],[1178.89,"And so if he misrepresents God, this is taking the name of the Lord in vain."],[1183.63,"This is a misrepresentation."],[1186.51,"And so he didn't portray God as holy."],[1190.19,"And so that's why this is this is significant."],[1195.11,"Then Moses spoke to the Lord, saying, Let the Lord, the God of the spirits of all flesh, appoint a man over the congregation who will go in and out before their face and lead them out and bring them in, then the congregation of the Lord will not be like sheep without a shepherd."],[1212.23,"So Moses says, Okay."],[1215.48,"Remember, this is part of Moses' repentance from what he did, is that he accepts the consequences."],[1220.6,"Okay, this is the consequence."],[1224.68,"That's part of the of repentance."],[1226.68,"Always, if we're still trying to evade, if we're apologizing, etc. to avoid, to try to avoid the consequences of what we've done, that is not repentance."],[1239.56,"That is trying to get ourselves out of trouble."],[1243.72,"That's trying to justify ourselves."],[1246.04,"Real repentance involves, okay, I accept what I've done."],[1249.72,"done I accept the consequences of what I have."],[1249.8,"Which Moses is doing"],[1255.44,"But Moses says, okay, I accept this consequence for me, but what does this mean for the people."],[1261.42,"Aaron's already gone."],[1263.1,"Miriam's already gone."],[1265.34,"When I'm gone, there needs to be someone to lead the people."],[1270.06,"People can't just be left out here in the wilderness."],[1274.88,"Wandering around like sheep without a shepherd."],[1278.0,"But he also uses these other phrases, someone to lead them out and bring them back in."],[1285.68,"This is not just a uh shepherding analogy"],[1292.17,"This is not just like, oh, you take the sheep out, you bring them back in"],[1295.57,"To lead them out and bring them back in, these are specific phrases that refer to going into battle and retreating or returning from battle."],[1307.25,"So they need not just any leader, but they're preparing specifically to go into the land, to go into the land of Canaan."],[1316.13,"There they are going to end up doing battle."],[1319.11,"And so they need someone."],[1321.69,"He's not implying that God won't."],[1323.53,"He's not saying he doesn't believe in God's promise that God will fight for them and lead them into battle."],[1329.45,"But they need someone to take his place, which is what?"],[1333.61,"They need some a leader who will represent God to them and them to God. in the context of the warfare they're about to make"],[1342.53,"This is going to be a difficult situation in all a whole variety of ways."],[1346.53,"Then the Lord spoke to Moses, saying, Take Joshua the son of Nun with you, a man who has the spirit in himself, and put your hand on him, and stand him before Eliezer the priest, and give orders to him before all the congregation, and give orders concerning him before them."],[1363.41,"You shall then give him give some of your glory to him, that the children of Israel may obey him."],[1369.89,"So he shall stand before Eliezer the priest, and they shall inquire before the Lord for him by the judgment of the Urim."],[1376.93,"At his word they shall go out, and at his word they shall come in."],[1381.01,"He and all the children of Israel in one accord, even all the congregation."],[1385.61,"So God says, okay, Joshua's that man."],[1388.01,"We've seen Joshua has been Moses' right hand man this whole time."],[1393.69,"Joshua has been in the tent of meeting when God is and Moses are speaking face to face."],[1399.13,"He went part way up the mountain with Moses."],[1402.17,"He was literally his right hand man when they were fighting the Amalekites, and Joshua had to hold one of his arms up."],[1410.97,"So Joshua's been there all along."],[1414.83,"And so he has seen all of this along with Moses."],[1420.11,"And this is why he's sort of chosen, to be the one to succeed Moses."],[1426.61,"And we see the same thing in the book of Acts"],[1432.65,"When they're choosing the deacons, when the apostles decide to choose an apostle to replace Judas."],[1439.78,"One of the criteria is we want someone who's been with us the whole time."],[1445.14,"They may not have been officially one of the twelve, or an apostle, but they've been with us the lot, they've witnessed these things, they've seen these things that have happened."],[1456.56,"They've been with us."],[1457.94,"And now are ready to step up into this role."],[1460.58,"Where they're getting that is this."],[1463.56,"That this is sort of what qualifies someone."],[1466.12,"So it goes beyond just like, you know, Moses was was mentoring him, you know, or or something like that."],[1474.9,"It's he because he's been an active participant all along, too."],[1479.94,"Remember, he's one of the spies who goes in and right."],[1482.58,"I mean, he's been doing things as well."],[1485.06,"Hasn't he also been in the tent sometimes of Moses?"],[1487.7,"Where yeah, you were outside, but yeah, that's yeah, he was in the tent of meeting when God and and Moses was speaking face to face."],[1495.23,"He's born witness to all of this."],[1496.91,"So he's sort of shared of the experiences that Moses has had."],[1500.03,"So Moses did as the Lord commanded him, and taking Joshua."],[1502.91,"He stood him before Eliezer the priest, and before all the congregation."],[1506.83,"Then he put his hands on him and appointed him as the Lord commanded Moses."],[1511.81,"So they go in front of everyone and in front of the high priest, he lays hands on him, that's how the authority is transferred."],[1518.89,"Now, a couple of notes in there from earlier, it talked about inquiring with the Urim."],[1524.17,"Remember, the Urim and the Thummim were kept on the breastplate of the high priest, and this was if they had to make a judgment call, and they honestly didn't know what to do, there was some way that they could throw these two stones that would indicate what to do."],[1540.65,"We don't know exactly what it was."],[1542.83,"We don't know if they had two colored sides or exactly how that all worked, but that was the way to sort of find out if you were honestly stumped."],[1551.07,"So it's saying Joshua is the one who will have the authority to make that call, that this is a situation in which we need to do this."],[1559.43,"Also, it says talks about Moses sharing some of his glory with him."],[1563.37,"Because Moses doesn't like give up his authority."],[1566.41,"It's not like, okay, I am now a retired prophet."],[1570.19,"Like I passed it on to you, and now, you know, I no longer speak for God, I no longer have any author."],[1576.6,"Obviously, not. God he are still speaking."],[1579.52,"And we're gonna see the whole book of Deuteronomy is Moses speaking authoritatively after this."],[1584.5,"So, yes. What what's the significance of like why would Joshua get to do the Urman Drum if it's on the like priests, like he keeps it, but he's he's the one who makes that call."],[1599.54,"It's when. When to use it."],[1600.6,"When to use it."],[1601.64,"You don't just use it whenever."],[1605.81,"And so Joshua, since he is gonna have he's gonna sit in Moses' seat."],[1610.13,"Okay. He's the final sort of court of appeal if there's a dispute or indecision."],[1617.7,"So he's he's acting more of a on the prophetic rule than like like a kingly."],[1623.79,"He's a prophet."],[1624.99,"Yeah. And he's so if he rules, he rules."],[1631.01,"On the thing."],[1631.95,"But if it goes to him and he isn't sure how to rule, that's when you use the Euromanthum."],[1639.47,"And yeah, it just kind of seems like it would be complete."],[1641.55,"You can't use it to bypass him."],[1644.34,"Like the priest can't just decide, you know, well, he hasn't said anything yet, but uh, you know, I'm gonna do this, and then go tell Joshua, well, this is what the Urim and Thum is."],[1654.44,"That's not how that works."],[1656.81,"Um, like I feel like um it's easier to kind of see Moses in this prophet role and then Joshua, I guess because of like the kind of commander aspect he has."],[1669.13,"Well, because of what he ends up doing, yeah."],[1671.37,"What is his role ends up being?"],[1673.61,"But the line between prophet and what get called judges, like in the book of judges, is very narrow."],[1681.93,"And Joshua sort of bridges that gap."],[1684.49,"Because, for example, the prophet Samuel.el is going to be referred to as the last judge of Isra"],[1690.45,"He doesn't go and do all the smiting that the judges do or that Joshua does, but he's called a judge."],[1695.57,"He's also a prophet."],[1697.35,"And so Joshua isn't going."],[1699.67,"He does a little bit, but he doesn't spend a lot of time making prophetic proclamations the way Moses did."],[1706.32,"He does a little, he's more his role is a little different in terms of what he ends up doing, but that's because of the time in the ministry he's called to."],[1715.46,"So different prophets are going to end up doing different, serving different roles and doing different things throughout the Old Testament."],[1722.72,"It's like the prophet Jonah's gonna get sent to a foreign city."],[1726.7,"Isaiah is gonna be sort of a court prophet for the king."],[1731.16,"Elijah is gonna be out in the wilderness sort of testifying against the king."],[1736.69,"Like so there's different I think that's helpful because we kind of I mean, usually it's you gotta think more of like John the Baptist and stuff, and then are somebody making like uh prophecies in the sense of like what's gonna happen in the future and right there's a lot more to it than just that."],[1755.73,"Yeah, yeah. Yeah, and so but notice here, the whole idea of the laying on of hands and succession is not this new thing that Roman Catholics make up in the Middle Ages."],[1769.11,"Sorry, Protestant friends."],[1771.65,"It's not even something that's new in the New Testament."],[1774.97,"This goes all the way back."],[1776.73,"Like Moses is the first person to ever have this kind of authority."],[1780.97,"And the way he transfers it to the next person is through the laying on of hands for succession."],[1787.69,"And Joshua is going to do the same thing."],[1796.57,"What we would now call ordination in our modern Anglicized Latin."],[1804.89,"There are these chains that run all through the Old Testament."],[1809.77,"And that start with Moses"],[1814.13,"So, not just the people we think of as biblical prophets and stuff, but the people who are going to show up as like rabbis in the New Testament and stuff have these chains of authority."],[1825.33,"If they have any authority, they have these chains going back to Moses."],[1828.53,"That's why uh Christ talks about the Pharisees sitting in Moses' seat."],[1833.97,"Remember, he says to his disciples, do whatever the Pharisees tell you because they sit in Moses' seat."],[1839.67,"They have this authority, and there's a chain connecting that Pharisee through the laying on of hands all the way back to Moses."],[1847.93,"So he sits in Moses' seat, do what he tells you."],[1850.33,"He has this authority, but don't do what he does because he's a hypocrite."],[1854.98,"Don't follow his way of life because his way of life doesn't match what he's saying."],[1860.42,"But the words he says do have that authority from God, and you should you should abide by it."],[1867.02,"Um but that's where that concept comes from."],[1870.3,"Now that gets cut off within Judaism."],[1874.22,"Because they have the view, they have the view all the way through the Second Temple period that for those ordnate quote unquote ordinations, those acts of laying out of hands to be valid, it has to happen in the land."],[1890.78,"So it has to happen in Judea."],[1894.32,"Essentially. That's why when the Pharisees show up in Galilee, even, like where Jesus is ministering in Galilee, the Pharisees show up there, it taught it says Pharisees came up from Judea."],[1905.52,"Paul goes to study in Jerusalem"],[1905.54,"Because if you were going to do that, that's why St."],[1911.22,"When he's studying to sort of become a rabbi with Gameliel."],[1915.56,"That was the only place you could do those ordinations."],[1917.64,"Well, guess what?"],[1918.92,"The Romans threw them all out at the end of the first century and wouldn't let them do any ordinations."],[1925.64,"So all of those chains got broken."],[1928.6,"So you can look this up."],[1930.04,"You can go to a you know an Orthodox Jewish website."],[1933.72,"They do have rabbis, the rabbis are ordained, but they distinguish what they mean by ordained with a modern rabbi and what ordination was before."],[1946.06,"Before that break."],[1947.74,"That it's not the same thing."],[1949.34,"And that's why authority functions differently in Orthodox Judaism now than it did then"],[1956.58,"There's not a person in Orthodox Judaism who could just like make a rule for all Jews the way Moses could or Joshua could or these other biblical figures, because those chains of authority got broken."],[1972.02,"And so now different rabbis can offer different opinions, and based on the perceived wisdom and holiness of that rabbi, Jewish people give more or less weight to those opinions."],[1984.27,"From different rabbis, but nobody can just say once and for all, this is it."],[1991.65,"So what we as Christians have historically believed is that the reason those all died is that Christ restarted it through the apostles."],[2004.75,"So when the apostles received the Holy Spirit at Pentecost, just like we read here, Joshua is someone who is indwelt with the Spirit."],[2013.92,"That Christ essentially sets up the apostles, and then the apostles then lay on hands to their successors, the next generation of bishops."],[2024.72,"This is what we call apostolic succession."],[2027.73,"Which again is not something that gets made up by the Roman Catholic Church of the Middle Ages, something that predates Christianity."],[2036.06,"But Christ sort of restarts these new chains with the apostles, with his disciples."],[2041.86,"And so we now have, you can go, like you go to the Patriarch of Antioch's website, you can read the list."],[2050.98,"There's this chain of patriarchs of Antioch going all the way back to Saint Peter."],[2056.34,"And then, of course, through St."],[2057.78,"Peter, Christ. But going back to the apostle who received that authority and who passed it on, to successors all the way down."],[2068.76,"So we have chains now, too."],[2072.34,"Within the church."],[2074.34,"But all this comes from the Torah."],[2077.46,"I think I said back when I did the introduction to the Torah, back when we first started Genesis, I think I said, and I still hold to the fact that you can get all of Christian theology just from the Torah."],[2088.54,"And so this is a prime example."],[2090.4,"This is something uh obscure that a lot of our Protestant friends, for example, would say isn't even in the Bible."],[2096.72,"But not only is in the Bible, but it's in the Torah."],[2101.84,"It's right here, this idea of authority being transferred in succession through the laying out of hands in a formal act."],[2109.6,"Um, how is this how much of this is related in the same way that they would be putting hands on the offerings?"],[2119.84,"Well, the laying out of hands is a way of setting something apart for a particular purpose, and that's the connection."],[2126.92,"So they lay hands on an animal before they sacrifice it because they're saying this goat, this sheep, this bull, whatever it is, is the one we're going to."],[2137.98,"And in some cases, like in the Day of Atonement, you have two goats."],[2141.34,"They get used for two different purposes."],[2143.02,"So you lay hands on the one and use it for the one purpose, and you lay hands on the other one and use it for the other."],[2147.51,"It's a way of designating and setting aside for a purpose."],[2151.85,"Remember, we also saw that I made because I made a snarky comment."],[2155.61,"Uh with the the whole tribe of the Levites."],[2160.97,"God had all the people of Israel gather and lay their hands on the Levites."],[2165.47,"And I said, This is because they're gonna kill all the Levites uh for their sins."],[2168.91,"Of course, obviously not"],[2169.35,"No. No, they're designating."],[2173.31,"Out of all of us, these people are the ones who are gonna have the purpose of serving in the tabernacle and later in the temple."],[2181.8,"They're being set apart for that purpose."],[2184.6,"So it's that's always that idea, being set apart for a purpose."],[2188.6,"In this case, it's to to wield authority."],[2191.96,"In that case, it's to be an offering."],[2194.46,"Or whatever. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2205.26,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2210.62,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.68,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: census, patchwork of genres","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Numbers 27</b>; little recap needed - left off with <b>census data</b> (\"Woo!\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Why census at beginning <b>and</b> end of Numbers: <b>succession of generations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">generation out of Egypt died in the wilderness</li><li class=\"d1\">new generation, their children, renumbered to <b>enter the land and face resistance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Numbers = \"<b>patchwork quilt</b>\" of material interspersed</li><li class=\"d1\">narrative, stories, census data, commandments - all mixed</li><li class=\"d0\">Switching genre now: <b>tiny piece of narrative</b> introduces a bunch of <b>legislative material</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then commandments for a while → another bit of narrative → commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">Only a few morsels of narrative left; <b>ten chapters left</b>, mostly commandments</li><li class=\"d1\">will go faster - \"not that quickly\" - won't finish ten tonight</li></ul>"},{"t":168.59,"lv":2,"h":"Numbers 27:1-2 - Daughters of Zelophehad","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Zelophehad</b>, son of Hepher, Gilead, Machir, Manasseh, son of Joseph</li><li class=\"d0\">Daughters: <b>Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, Tirzah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Repeat of the joke: name your daughter <b>Hoglah</b> and \"she will grow up and harm you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I will not baptize your daughter Hogla\" - will change the name, \"she will thank me\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Milkah</b> or <b>Tirzah</b> fine</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Son of, son of, son of\" = <b>not a complete list</b> of generations</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>locating them</b> within the tribe, clan, extended family, immediate family</li><li class=\"d1\">just in <b>ascending</b> order instead of descending</li></ul>"},{"t":255.02,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: names as identity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: is it about <b>leadership, importance</b>? not just \"locating\"?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: part of <b>identity</b> - we don't have that specificity in American culture</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Roman citizens</b> had five or six names</li><li class=\"d1\">given name, sometimes lame: <b>Primus, Secundus, Tertius, Quadratus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then father's, grandfather's, an ancestor's name</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ethiopia</b> - given name, father's name, grandfather's name</li><li class=\"d1\">identity = <b>tracing the line back</b> into larger units</li><li class=\"d0\">US: first name + last name, \"about as far as it works here\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Traditional cultures: identity is a function of being part of <b>larger units</b>, present and historical</li><li class=\"d0\">So: \"who are these women coming to see Moses?\" - here's their family, clan, tribe</li></ul>"},{"t":384.9,"lv":3,"h":"Gateway of the tabernacle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">They stood before <b>Moses, Eliezer the priest</b>, rulers, congregation, at the <b>door of the tabernacle</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tabernacle</b> = the tent; <b>curtain</b> around the perimeter</li><li class=\"d1\">non-priests/Levites didn't go in</li><li class=\"d0\">Place where all gather: Moses has something to say, major dispute, God addresses all the people</li><li class=\"d0\">Later becomes the <b>city gate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">city folk + farmers go there for business, where the <b>elders</b> sit</li><li class=\"d1\">contracts - <b>witnesses</b> there</li><li class=\"d1\">disputes - elders settle</li><li class=\"d0\">In the wilderness camp = the <b>gateway of the tabernacle</b></li></ul>"},{"t":485.69,"lv":2,"h":"Numbers 27:3-4 - The daughters' case","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Our father died in the desert\" - <b>not in Korah's company</b>, died \"in his own sin\", <b>no sons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first generation out of Egypt; not in Korah or other rebellions - died with <b>that whole generation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No sons = <b>end of his line, end of his inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Inheritance = land</b> - each family gets a piece in <b>Canaan</b>, their family's land</li><li class=\"d1\">hypothetically reverts to the family in the <b>Jubilee</b> every 50 years</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"dad's clothes, walking stick, tent\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"What happens to us?\" - sons of the next generation receive land, they have none</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chutzpah</b> - technically <b>women had no right to address court cases</b> then</li><li class=\"d0\">Ask: don't let his name be wiped out; <b>give a possession among our father's brothers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">uncles have male cousins who'll inherit; \"we would like a piece just like them\"</li></ul>"},{"t":626.3,"lv":2,"h":"Numbers 27:5-11 - The Lord's ruling","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses brings their case <b>before the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Lord: \"The daughters of Zelophehad <b>speak correctly</b>\" - give them the possession among their father's brothers</li><li class=\"d0\">Law for Israel - inheritance order:</li><li class=\"d1\">no son → to the <b>daughter</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no daughter → his <b>brother</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no brothers → his <b>father's brother</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no father's brothers → <b>closest relative in his tribe</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"An <b>ordinance of judgment</b> to the children of Israel\"</li></ul>"},{"t":671.38,"lv":3,"h":"Not \"girl power\": separate family inheritances","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: point of this? \"Early feminism\" - \"sort of\"</li><li class=\"d1\">women do inherit what normally they couldn't</li><li class=\"d1\">but not just women: no kids → brother → uncle</li><li class=\"d0\">Idea: preserve <b>this particular piece of land as a family inheritance</b> - theoretically</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Separate</b> - father's brother's land came from <b>his</b> father, the grandfather</li><li class=\"d1\">land didn't keep subdividing; inherited by <b>firstborn</b></li><li class=\"d1\">second son inheriting a piece keeps it separate</li><li class=\"d0\">God preventing another route to accumulation</li><li class=\"d1\">arranging marriages, <b>levirate marriage</b>, social customs → \"cobble together this huge\" holding that never leaves my family</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Flies in the face of the <b>year of Jubilee</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":770.3,"lv":3,"h":"Generational poverty vs. generational wealth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jubilee's problem isn't <b>generational wealth</b> - it's <b>generational poverty</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a horrible thing if a family prospers across generations</li><li class=\"d1\">horrible if <b>other families</b> go generation after generation <b>without land of their own</b>, no way to get it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"born into poverty, live your whole life in it, nothing you can do\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Also a <b>moral hazard</b> to being born wealthy with no way to lose it - \"does not tend to produce the best sort of people\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>less important</b> in the Torah than the opposite</li><li class=\"d0\">Make bad choices → poverty for the rest of your life, but <b>shouldn't doom children and grandchildren</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jubilee designed to prevent that</li><li class=\"d0\">So ruling is \"not so much about <b>girl power</b>\" as <b>keeping inheritances separate</b>, preventing accumulation at other families' expense</li></ul>"},{"t":886.24,"lv":2,"h":"Numbers 27:12-14 - Moses to see the land","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Lord: go up <b>Mount Nebo</b>, see <b>Canaan</b>, \"which I give to the children of Israel\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Already known: Moses won't enter - <b>struck the rock</b> instead of speaking to it</li><li class=\"d0\">They're in the <b>plains of Moab</b>, across the Jordan - from the top he can look down on the land spread out</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Then you shall see it and be <b>added to your people</b>\" - as <b>Aaron</b> on <b>Mount Hor</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reason: transgressed God's word in the <b>desert of Zin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">failed to <b>sanctify</b> God before the congregation</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>waters of contention at Kadesh</b> - \"in case it's been a couple of months\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God literally: go see the land <b>because you're going to die</b>, as Aaron did</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses made aware: \"this is it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":966.51,"lv":3,"h":"\"Added to your people\": an afterlife","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not just a <b>euphemism</b> for death</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>His people</b>? Not Israel - he's already part of it</li><li class=\"d1\">= ancestors, <b>Aaron</b>, <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"some kind of <b>community</b> with them\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Implies an <b>afterlife</b> - nod to <b>Bart Ehrman</b> and certain modern secular Jews</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't mean added to the <b>tomb</b></li><li class=\"d1\">patriarchs are all buried in the same tombs</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Moses isn't added to any</b> of their tombs → joining them \"where they are\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't necessarily imply <b>bodily resurrection</b> - can't be that specific</li><li class=\"d1\">but death is <b>not the end</b> of Moses' existence or his ancestors'</li><li class=\"d0\">Naming <b>Aaron</b> implies he didn't cease to exist a few chapters ago</li></ul>"},{"t":1063.18,"lv":3,"h":"Moses' sin: misrepresenting God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">120+ years old - \"eventually he was gonna die\" - but reason he can't enter is the <b>transgression</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Failed to <b>sanctify God</b> before the congregation = failed to treat God as <b>holy</b> before them</li><li class=\"d1\">angry, struck the rock</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Moses' job as prophet</b>: <b>represent God to the people</b> and <b>the people to God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God to people: speak with his authority <b>and accurately represent who God is and what he says</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people to God: <b>prayer and intercession</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Hit a rock with a stick\" - like throwing something on the floor in anger?</li><li class=\"d1\">for Moses, <b>misrepresenting God</b> to the people = very serious</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the way the people know who God is, is Moses\"</li><li class=\"d0\">= <b>taking the name of the Lord in vain</b> - didn't portray God as holy</li></ul>"},{"t":1195.11,"lv":2,"h":"Numbers 27:15-17 - Moses asks for a successor","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses: let the Lord, <b>God of the spirits of all flesh</b>, appoint a man over the congregation</li><li class=\"d1\">one to \"<b>go in and out</b> before their face\", <b>lead out and bring in</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so they're not \"<b>sheep without a shepherd</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Part of Moses' <b>repentance</b> - he <b>accepts the consequence</b></li><li class=\"d1\">apologizing to avoid consequences is <b>not repentance</b> - \"trying to get ourselves out of trouble\", justify ourselves</li><li class=\"d1\">real repentance: \"I accept what I've done, I accept the consequences\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Concern now: <b>what does this mean for the people</b>? Aaron gone, Miriam gone</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Lead out and bring in\" = <b>not just shepherding</b></li><li class=\"d1\">specific phrases for <b>going into battle and returning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not doubting God will fight for them - but need a leader to <b>represent God to them and them to God</b> in the coming <b>warfare</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1346.53,"lv":2,"h":"Numbers 27:18-21 - Joshua chosen","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Lord: take <b>Joshua son of Nun</b>, \"a man who has the <b>Spirit</b> in himself\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>lay your hand on him</b>, stand him before <b>Eliezer</b>, commission him before all</li><li class=\"d1\">give him <b>some of your glory</b> so Israel obeys</li><li class=\"d0\">He inquires through <b>Eliezer</b> by the <b>Urim</b>; \"at his word they go out, at his word they come in\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Joshua: Moses' <b>right-hand man</b> all along</li><li class=\"d1\">in the <b>tent of meeting</b> when God and Moses spoke face to face</li><li class=\"d1\">went part way up the mountain; held up Moses' arm vs. the <b>Amalekites</b>; one of the <b>spies</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same pattern in <b>Acts</b> - choosing deacons, replacing <b>Judas</b></li><li class=\"d1\">criterion: someone <b>with us the whole time</b>, witnessed these things</li><li class=\"d1\">more than mentoring - an <b>active participant</b> who shared Moses' experiences</li></ul>"},{"t":1500.03,"lv":3,"h":"Numbers 27:22-23 - Laying on of hands; Urim and Thummim","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses does it: Joshua before <b>Eliezer</b> and the congregation, <b>hands laid on</b> = authority transferred</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Urim and Thummim</b> - kept on the high priest's <b>breastplate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for a judgment call when they honestly don't know</li><li class=\"d1\">some way of throwing two stones; \"don't know exactly\" - maybe two colored sides</li><li class=\"d1\">way to find out \"if you were honestly stumped\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So Joshua has authority to say <b>when this is a situation for it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moses <b>shares some of his glory</b> - doesn't give up authority</li><li class=\"d1\">not a \"retired prophet\"; still speaks for God</li><li class=\"d1\">all of <b>Deuteronomy</b> = Moses speaking authoritatively afterward</li></ul>"},{"t":1584.5,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Urim and Thummim; Joshua as prophet and judge","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: why Joshua gets to use it if the priest keeps it?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: <b>when</b> to use it - \"you don't just use it whenever\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Joshua sits in <b>Moses' seat</b> - final <b>court of appeal</b> in a dispute</li><li class=\"d1\">he rules; if he can't, then Urim and Thummim</li><li class=\"d1\">priest <b>can't bypass</b> him</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Moses = prophet; Joshua = commander?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: line between <b>prophet and judge</b> is very narrow - Joshua bridges it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Samuel</b> = prophet and \"last judge of Israel\", no smiting</li><li class=\"d1\">Joshua little prophesying - role differs by <b>time and ministry</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Different prophets, different roles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jonah</b> sent to a foreign city; <b>Isaiah</b> court prophet; <b>Elijah</b> in the wilderness vs. the king</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophet isn't only <b>predicting the future</b> (John the Baptist type)</li></ul>"},{"t":1755.73,"lv":2,"h":"Laying on of hands: succession since Moses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not something \"Roman Catholics make up in the Middle Ages\" - \"sorry, Protestant friends\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not new in the NT either - goes back to <b>Moses</b>, first with this authority</li><li class=\"d1\">transfers it by <b>laying on of hands</b>; Joshua does the same</li><li class=\"d0\">= what we now call <b>ordination</b>, \"in our modern Anglicized Latin\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chains</b> run through the OT, starting with Moses</li><li class=\"d1\">also rabbis of the NT: any authority = chain back to Moses</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Christ says Pharisees <b>sit in Moses' seat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"do whatever they tell you\" - authority connected through laying on of hands back to Moses</li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't do what he does\" - <b>hypocrite</b>, way of life doesn't match</li><li class=\"d1\">but words have God's authority</li></ul>"},{"t":1870.3,"lv":3,"h":"Chain broken in Judaism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Cut off within Judaism</li><li class=\"d0\">Second Temple view: <b>ordinations valid only in the land</b>, in <b>Judea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why Pharisees \"came up from Judea\" to <b>Galilee</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why <b>Paul</b> went to study in Jerusalem with <b>Gamaliel</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans threw them out</b> at end of 1st century, no ordinations → <b>chains broken</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox Jewish rabbis are \"ordained\" - but distinguish modern ordination from <b>before the break</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Authority works differently now</li><li class=\"d1\">no one can make a rule <b>for all Jews</b> as Moses or Joshua could</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbis offer different opinions, weighed by perceived <b>wisdom and holiness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"nobody can just say once and for all, this is it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1991.65,"lv":3,"h":"Apostolic succession","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christian view: <b>Christ restarted it through the apostles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">at <b>Pentecost</b> the apostles receive the Holy Spirit - as <b>Joshua</b> is indwelt with the Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">apostles lay hands on successors, next generation of <b>bishops</b></li><li class=\"d0\">= <b>apostolic succession</b> - not Middle Ages Roman Catholic invention; <b>predates Christianity</b></li><li class=\"d0\">New chains with the apostles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Patriarch of Antioch's website</b> lists the chain back to <b>St. Peter</b>, then Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">\"All this comes from the Torah\" - can get all Christian theology from the <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"obscure\" - Protestants say it isn't in the Bible; it's in the <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">authority transferred in succession by a <b>formal act of laying on of hands</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2101.84,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: laying on hands on offerings and Levites","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: related to laying hands on the <b>offerings</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: laying on of hands = <b>setting apart for a particular purpose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hands on the animal: \"<b>this</b> goat, sheep, bull is the one\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Day of Atonement</b>: two goats, two purposes - hands on each for its own</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Levites</b> - all Israel laid hands on them</li><li class=\"d1\">earlier snarky comment: \"they're gonna kill all the Levites for their sins\" - obviously not</li><li class=\"d1\">designating: \"out of all of us, these are the ones\" to serve in the <b>tabernacle</b> and <b>temple</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Always <b>set apart for a purpose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">here: to <b>wield authority</b></li><li class=\"d1\">there: to be an <b>offering</b></li></ul>"}]});
